finding: Independent review of origin/main...38a264a9 (HEAD 38a264a928b319472b1f5b2ea4957d2656eaa299), stage 1. Examined the unit and CONTRIBUTING in full; Bevy's three requested shaders in full and gpu.rs module docs; cluster shaders, layouts, allocation/reset/dispatch path, oracle, World.abortDrawGroup, and the fixed-wait-removal diff and profiler readback failure path. Ran cluster.gpu.test.ts independently: passes, 12 assertions. Also ran an independent GPU boundary reproduction with the repository TypeGPU preload and setupGlobals (no source edits). Findings: 1. src/standard/rendering/cluster.ts:644-646: the first depth slice is floor(log(nearest/near)/log(far/near)*24). A sphere tangent to a slice boundary must also belong to the preceding slice under lightClusters' inclusive sphere/AABB test, but that slice is never rasterized. Reproduced with perspective camera near=1, far=4096, origin camera, light center=(0,0,-65), range=1: clusterIndex(7,4,11)=1715 is present in lightClusters(), but GPU grid count is 0. This silently drops a membership without overflow. 2. src/standard/rendering/cluster.gpu.test.ts:47-55, 134-146: the oracle varies only X/Y at world Z=0 with both cameras at Z=5. It does not exercise behind-camera lights, near/far straddles, corners/top/bottom edges, different view transforms, pool overflow, or a zero-light frame after a populated frame (zero is first). No count/populate mutation evidence is recorded in the commits. Its passing result does not establish the requested coverage; the reproduced depth-boundary defect passes it. 3. src/standard/rendering/cluster.ts:740-791: rasterBounds scans all 16 X and 9 Y tile intervals separately for each depth slice and expands by a tile, rather than Bevy's calculate_sphere_cluster_bounds projected sphere box. This is a substantive unnamed divergence: neither the unit nor commit messages identify the replacement algorithm and its reason. Retaining Shallot's existing logarithmic ortho substrate is understandable, but the new per-slice XY scan is not that substrate. This is a faithfulness/documentation finding, not a speed judgment. 4. src/extras/profile/readback.oracle.ts:40-43: the removed deadline leaves a polling path with no validation-error exit. onSubmittedWorkDone may resolve after invalid work; Profile.resolve's rejection handler at src/extras/profile/index.ts:432-435 only logs and frees the slot. A validation failure preventing timestamp delivery leaves gpuTime without readback-witness, and the oracle keeps stepping/polling rather than rejecting (until the external test hang ceiling). Device loss is raced; validation failure is not. This fails the requested every-waiting-path settlement claim. 5. src/standard/rendering/cluster.ts:470: projection dirty state is committed before the AABB dispatch is submitted. If projection changes and a later draw system throws, World.abortDrawGroup (src/engine/ecs/world.ts:235-255) discards the grid dispatch but not clusters.last or the immediate projection-buffer write. The next frame with the same projection skips the grid rebuild and bins against stale AABBs. This is carried from the previous implementation, but still fails the expressly requested aborted-draw state claim for this stage. Notes: per-stage storage visibility matches the reported counts and vertex bindings are read-only. Local/global scan barriers appear uniform; scratch length is divisible by 256. Pool writes are clamped and overflow header is still written/read through the existing diagnostic seam. Z-slice capacity is exactly 256*24 per slot; the clamped compact count and at-most-24 loop bound make its unguarded write bounded under normal frame resets. No readback resize was added. Normal active-view frames clear compact counters, pool headers, all scratch counters, and all indirect instance counts before dispatch, including zero-light frames. Remaining uncertainty: did not rerun CI, mutation checks, loss/validation injection, snapshot/restore, or hardware-specific lifecycle tests. The local adapter did not report identity, so this run is not a Metal hardware qualification. Speed was not judged. No code edited.
